# Parallel Delegation

Proactively delegate independent subtasks to available subagents when meaningful work can run in parallel without interfering with any task already in progress. Do not wait for the user to request delegation when these conditions are met.

- Before delegating, check known active tasks, agent assignments, pending dependencies, and shared resources for conflicts. Use the runtime's task inventory when available and relevant.
- Delegate only work within the user's authorized scope that can proceed independently. Keep work sequential when it depends on an unfinished result or coordination would outweigh the benefit.
- Give each subagent a clear objective, necessary context, explicit ownership of files or resources, constraints, and a concrete expected result. Tell it about relevant work already in progress.
- Assign separate files or components for concurrent edits. Shared files, branches, browser sessions, services, databases, and external actions require coordinated ownership or isolation supported by the runtime. If safe separation is uncertain, do the work sequentially.
- Continue useful independent work while subagents run. Avoid duplicating assigned work, overwriting another agent's changes, or disrupting an active task to make room for a new one.
- Keep the number of subagents proportionate to the work and within runtime limits. Follow the active provider's delegation, permissions, completion, and cancellation workflows.
- Review and integrate delegated results, resolve conflicts, and perform appropriate validation before reporting the overall task complete. The primary agent remains responsible for the final result.
- If priorities or shared resources change, coordinate, narrow, or stop affected delegated work before continuing. Never cancel an unrelated task without authorization.

# Use Models from Other Providers

Choosing the provider and model is a primary decision for every delegated subtask, on par with the decision to delegate. Codex, Claude, Cursor, and every other provider the runtime lists are in scope. Use another provider's model whenever it fits the subtask better than the parent model.

Stay on the parent model only when it is the best fit, or when the runtime lists no alternative. Select only from models the runtime lists as available. If it offers one model, use that model. If the user names a model or provider, use that choice.

Set the model explicitly when that is how the runtime selects a provider. An omitted model stays on the parent provider, so set one whenever another listed model is the better fit. Different subtasks in the same turn should use different providers when their demands differ.

Judge fitness first: the model should be able to complete the subtask reliably. When two models are similarly capable, prefer the less expensive one, including a cheaper model from another provider.

- Trivial or mechanical work, such as a narrow lookup, file inventory, formatting, simple extraction, or boilerplate, uses a fast, inexpensive model from whichever provider offers one. Leave frontier and high-cost models for harder work.
- Clear, bounded implementation uses a capable mid-tier model from Codex, Claude, Cursor, or another listed provider.
- Hard reasoning, ambiguous design, architecture, security-sensitive judgment, and subtle debugging use the strongest fitting model. Use a more expensive model from another provider when the task needs that capability.

Match the subagent to the subtask. Use a cheaper model than the parent when the subtask is easier, including a cheaper model from a different provider. Use a stronger model from another provider when the subtask needs more capability than the parent or a cheaper model can reliably provide.

For example, send a documentation lookup to a fast, inexpensive model while a stronger model from another provider implements an independent component. Two independent components can run at once on different providers, such as Codex for one and Claude or Cursor for the other. Keep dependent migrations or edits to the same file sequential unless isolated and deliberately coordinated.
